Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor and enforce tenancy boundaries at scale?
- How would you test onboarding/offboarding and policy drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests proficiency designing fine-grained, scalable access controls for multi-tenant data lakes using ABAC, tags, and policy syntax.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tags and aws:PrincipalTag
- Resource policies with aws:RequestTag and stringEquals conditions
- S3 bucket prefixes per tenant and least privilege
- Testing cross-tenant access and tag revocation

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (IAM role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy (alternative strict guard)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:ExistingObjectTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tags and ensure no stale access remains?
- How would you integrate with CI to validate policies in a sandbox before prod?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, compliant multi-tenant real-time pipeline with data residency requirements, cross-account access, and disaster recovery.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant data locality
- Exactly-once delivery semantics across streaming and storage
- Cross-account IAM with STS and least privilege
- DR planning across regions, cost-aware choices
- Observability with CloudWatch, X-Ray

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: idempotent event handler skeleton
async function handleEvent(evt){
  const key = evt.tenant_id + ':' + evt.event_id;
  const exists = await db.get(key);
  if(exists) return; // idempotent
  await db.put(key, true);
  // process
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate RPO/RTO and perform canary DR tests?
- What are trade-offs between KDS and MSK in cost, latency, and reliability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account IAM and S3 permissions, a common real-world pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account roles and trust policies
- Bucket policies and principal scoping
- Least privilege and auditability
- Testing: assume role and perform PutObject

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy for cross-account role in Account B
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/LambdaExecutionRole" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Inline policy for cross-account role allowing PutObject
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Action":["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b-name/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Bucket policy in Account B
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Principal":{"AWS":"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/LambdaExecutionRole"},
      "Action":"s3:PutObject",
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b-name/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify if the Lambda runs in a VPC without internet access?
- How would you enforce per-object ACLs or S3 object tagging for billing separation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of private AWS data access patterns, policy composition, and practical testing. 

## Key Concepts
- S3 public access blocking and bucket policy conditions
- VPC Endpoints and endpoint policies
- aws:SourceVpce condition and VPCE validation
- IAM roles for data producers

## Code Example
```
# Bucket policy (allow via VPCE)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::landing-logs/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-abc123"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```
# VPCE policy (minimal, allows basic access to bucket)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::landing-logs/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```
```
# IAM role for producer (trusts producer principal)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {"Effect": "Allow","Action": ["s3:PutObject"],"Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::landing-logs/*"}
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to multiple VPCEs per environment?
- How would you monitor and audit VPCE access over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-region, multi-account DR with strict IAM boundaries and automated failover.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-region replication (S3 CRR, DynamoDB Global Tables)
- Route 53 failover with health checks
- Per-tenant IAM roles with least privilege and explicit denies
- IaC automation and DR testing

## Code Example

```terraform
# Minimal Terraform snippet for Route53 failover
resource "aws_route53_health_check" "region_a" {
  fqdn = "api.example.com"
  type = "HTTPS"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary" {
  name = "api.example.com"
  type = "A"
  zone_id = var.zone_id
  set_identifier = "RegionA"
  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "primary"
    health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.region_a.id
  }
  alias {
    name = aws_lb.region_a.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_lb.region_a.zone_id
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "secondary" {
  name = "api.example.com"
  type = "A"
  zone_id = var.zone_id
  set_identifier = "RegionB"
  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "secondary"
    health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.region_b.id
  }
  alias {
    name = aws_lb.region_b.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_lb.region_b.zone_id
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify data consistency after failover?
- What monitoring alerts would you add?
